    "lawCode": "Amd §§70, 83, 89-h & 100, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A4904\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public service law, in relation to\ntransfer of franchises or stocks\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would expand the types of business entities that must obtain\nPublic Service Commission (PSC) approval for the transfer of franchises\nor stocks of electric, gas, water, steam and telephone corporations.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSections 1, 2, 3 and 4 of this bill amend Public Service Law (PSL) §§ 70\n(gas corporations and electric corporations), 83 (steam corporations),\n89-h (water corporations) and 100 (telephone corporations), respective-\nly, to expand the PSC's jurisdiction over transfers of franchises or\nstocks to include companies, associations, partnerships and other busi-\nness entities that propose to acquire such franchises or stocks.\n\nSection 5 of this bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nPSL §§70, 83, 89-h and 100 establish requirements for the transfer of\nfranchises and stocks of electric, gas, steam, water or telephone corpo-\nrations, respectively. Such provisions generally prohibit the transfer\nor lease of franchises or stocks to stock corporations without PSC\nreview and approval.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new proposal.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nThe PSL provides that the PSC must approve any transfer of franchises or\nstock of electric, gas, steam, water or telephone corporations to stock\ncorporations. However, entities formed as partnerships, limited liabil-\nity companies and associations, as well as other business entities that\nare not stock corporations, do not fall within the purview of the\nfranchise/stock acquisition provisions of the PSL.  Therefore, the PSC\ndoes not have jurisdiction over acquisitions of stock by and transfers\nof franchises to such entities as they are not \"stock corporations.\"\n\nThis lack of jurisdiction could result in a transfer of control of a\nutility company, and possibly detrimental consequences to the utility\nand its customers, particularly where the interests of shareholders,\npartners or members might not coincide with those of the utility and\nutility customers. The reliability of the various utility systems is\nintegral to the personal and economic needs of the residential and\ncommercial utility customers in this State. It is essential that the PSC\n\ncontinue to review proposals to transfer stock or franchises to ensure\nthat purchasers have the experience and ability to maintain utility\nsystems in a safe and reliable manner and provide service at just and\nreasonable rates.\n\nThis bill closes this loophole by providing PSC with jurisdiction to\nreview and determine whether stock acquisitions over a ten per centum\nthreshold by all business entities should be approved. This expanded\njurisdiction is thus consistent with PSC's mandate to ensure that such\ntransfer is in the public interest and that safe and reliable utility\nservice is provided at just and reasonable rates.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis bill takes effect upon enactment.\n\n",
